Do not treat a claimed framebuffer as a conflict when there is no PCI at all.

xf86PostProbe() refuses to continue if a framebuffer slot was claimed
alongside a bus slot, because the server then cannot tell which device drives
the screen and asks the user to disambiguate with busIDs.  Without
XSERVER_LIBPCIACCESS the other half of that test is hardcoded to TRUE:

    if (fbSlotClaimed && (
    #ifdef XSERVER_LIBPCIACCESS
                             pciSlotClaimed
    #else
                             TRUE
    #endif
        ))
        FatalError("Cannot run in framebuffer mode. Please specify busIDs ...");

so claiming a framebuffer is unconditionally fatal.  The driver opens the
device, logs "using /dev/fb0", and the server then dies telling the user to
specify busIDs for a bus that does not exist.

That TRUE reads "no libpciaccess means an older server that reaches PCI some
other way" -- the same assumption xf86-video-fbdev makes about the Resource
Access Control interface, and wrong for the same reason.  It predates a modern
server built with PCI support switched off entirely.

With no PCI there is no bus slot to conflict with, so nothing conflicts.

--- a/hw/xfree86/common/xf86Bus.c
+++ b/hw/xfree86/common/xf86Bus.c
@@ -565,6 +565,32 @@
 #endif
 #ifdef XSERVER_LIBPCIACCESS
                              pciSlotClaimed
+#elif defined(__LIKEOS__)
+                             /* Nothing can conflict with the framebuffer here.
+                              *
+                              * The test is meant to catch an ambiguous
+                              * configuration: a framebuffer slot claimed
+                              * alongside a bus slot, where the server cannot
+                              * tell which device drives the screen, so the
+                              * user is asked to disambiguate with busIDs.
+                              *
+                              * The `TRUE` below reads "no libpciaccess means an
+                              * older server that reaches PCI another way", and
+                              * that is the same assumption the fbdev driver
+                              * makes about the Resource Access Control
+                              * interface.  It predates a modern server built
+                              * with PCI support switched off entirely, which is
+                              * what this is: there is no bus slot to conflict
+                              * with, and no busID to specify even if one
+                              * wanted to.
+                              *
+                              * Left as TRUE it makes claiming a framebuffer
+                              * unconditionally fatal -- the driver opens
+                              * /dev/fb0, reports "using /dev/fb0", and the
+                              * server then dies telling the user to specify
+                              * busIDs for a bus that does not exist.
+                              */
+                             FALSE
 #else
                              TRUE
 #endif
